From 6111586ceecb44f26871ac7014dea4f3bcbcb83b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 22:59:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 21/24] l2r-local: entry 23-m: drop stack: steps, depth and
 drain for a host runtime

A host runtime that frees its own containers shares the thread's stack, so
that one drain pops its work and the glue's in one order, last deferred
first, and one end work follows both. New: a step (defer_step,
run_step), incremental work that stays on the stack until it answers that
it is done, run again after what it deferred meanwhile (a host's array
free in two passes, one element at a time); depth(), the number of
entries, so that a step can tell whether a release it made deferred work;
drain(), which runs what is pending and returns at once inside a drain
(the outermost one runs whatever is pushed meanwhile). A step is
`extern "C"`, as a release: one that panics aborts instead of unwinding
out of the drain with the thread still draining. A step that is done
leaves from under what it pushed. Outside a drain the stack stays empty
(checked in debug builds). Tests: a step yielding to its deferrals, a step
done with work above it, a step deferred inside a drain, an empty drain.
